.TH PATHAUDIT 1 "2026-07-26" "pathaudit 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pathaudit \(em scan PATH directory roots for hazards
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pathaudit
.RI [ \-\- ]
.I ROOT...
.P
.B pathaudit \-\-path
.P
.B pathaudit \-\-command
.I NAME
.P
.B pathaudit \-\-help
.P
.B pathaudit \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pathaudit
is a small ISO C17 command\-line scanner for risks in PATH directory entries.
It supports three exclusive invocation modes.
.P
Explicit\-root mode is
.BR "pathaudit [\-\-] ROOT..." .
Every inspection root is named as a command\-line operand.
In that mode the process
.B PATH
environment variable is ignored.
.P
Opt\-in
.B pathaudit \-\-path
reads
.B PATH
exactly once, splits the value on ASCII colon
.RB ( : )
only, and classifies each resulting component with the same hazard rules used
for explicit roots.
Empty components created by leading, trailing, or consecutive colons are
retained.
Duplicate components are retained in their original positions.
If
.B PATH
is unset,
.B \-\-path
is reject\-closed: stdout remains empty, stderr reports
.BR PATH_UNSET ,
and the exit status is
.BR 2 .
If
.B PATH
is set to an empty string, the mode treats that value as exactly one empty
component
.RB ( EMPTY_ROOT )
and exits with status
.B 1
when inspection completes.
An existing component that is not a directory
(regular file, symlink\-to\-file, or
.B ENOTDIR
through a non\-directory component) reports
.B NON_DIRECTORY_ROOT
as a completed hazard (status
.BR 1 ),
distinct from a usable directory and from
.BR MISSING_ROOT .
.P
Opt\-in
.B pathaudit \-\-command
.I NAME
reads
.B PATH
the same way as
.BR \-\-path ,
then walks components in resolution order for one command basename only.
It emits
.B MATCH
lines for regular executable candidates of that basename, then applicable
hazard lines from the shared taxonomy.
It does not flood stdout with unrelated executables that merely share a PATH
directory
.RI ( "benign basename collisions" ).
Unset
.B PATH
is reject\-closed exactly as for
.BR \-\-path .
.P
Explicit\-root mode does not search for executables and remains ownership\-blind.
.B \-\-path
scans top\-level regular executables for shadowing and for the shared
executable trust model;
.B \-\-command
applies that same trust model to each
.B MATCH
target.
None of the modes walk nested directory trees or remediate anything.
Under
.B \-\-path
and
.BR \-\-command ,
usable directory components may also emit
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
for the component
.B realpath
and each canonical ancestor through
.B /
when the directory owner is neither UID 0 nor the invoking real UID;
under
.B \-\-command
that directory/ancestor walk is subject to the same match\-or\-plant\-risk
applicability gate as directory permission codes.
Shared offending ancestors keep the lowest PATH index.
One root or one PATH component is one inspection input; duplicates remain
distinct.
Inspection is read\-only and requires no special privilege.
Classification uses the root bytes and one
.BR stat (2) \-equivalent
lookup of the final target
.RB ( \-\-path
and
.B \-\-command
also probe executable candidates and may
.BR realpath (3)
matches).
Ordering and escaping are byte\-oriented and locale\-independent.
.P
This narrow slice does not inspect packages, processes, services, capabilities,
ACLs, or mount options.
Ownership uses the shared
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
trust rule (UID 0 or the invoking real UID) on executable targets resolved by
.B \-\-path
and
.BR \-\-command ,
and on usable PATH\-directory
.B realpath
chains under those modes as described above.
It performs no remediation, networking, persistence, monitoring, or daemon work.
Filesystem state can change concurrently, so a result describes metadata
observed during that invocation and is not a security lock.
This manual does not claim that
.B pathaudit
is released.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing so a root may begin with
.BR \- .
.TP
.B \-\-path
Read the process
.B PATH
environment variable once, split on
.BR : ,
and audit each component.
Accepts no root operands and no other options.
Combining
.B \-\-path
with any
.I ROOT
operand, with
.BR \-\-command ,
with another non\-informational option, or with a second
.B \-\-path
is a usage error.
.TP
.BI \-\-command " NAME"
Walk process
.B PATH
in resolution order for the single command basename
.IR NAME .
Requires exactly one
.I NAME
operand and accepts no roots or other options.
.I NAME
must be nonempty and must not contain
.BR / ;
otherwise stderr reports
.B INVALID_COMMAND
with the escaped operand and the exit status is
.B 2
with empty stdout.
Leading\-dash names are accepted as
.I NAME
(for example
.BR \-\-command \-tool ).
Combining
.B \-\-command
with roots,
.BR \-\-path ,
extra operands, or a missing
.I NAME
is a usage error.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the usage summary to standard output and exit with status
.BR 0 .
Extra operands are a usage error.
A stdout write or flush failure returns status
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.B pathaudit 0.1.0
to standard output and exit with status
.BR 0 .
Extra operands are a usage error.
A stdout write or flush failure returns status
.BR 2 .
.SH PATH HAZARDS
The closed hazard taxonomy for this slice is shared by explicit roots, by every
.B \-\-path
component, and by applicable
.B \-\-command
components:
.TP
.B EMPTY_ROOT
The operand or PATH component has zero bytes.
It is not translated to the current directory and receives no filesystem lookup.
An explicitly empty
.B PATH
yields exactly one
.B EMPTY_ROOT
finding (status
.BR 1 ),
which must not be confused with unset
.B PATH
.RB ( PATH_UNSET ,
status
.BR 2 ).
.TP
.B RELATIVE_ROOT
The nonempty input does not begin with
.BR / ,
including
.B .
and
.BR .. .
The input is still looked up relative to the process working directory, so it
may also receive a resolution or permission finding.
.TP
.B MISSING_ROOT
Target lookup reports
.B ENOENT
(including a dangling final symlink).
.TP
.B NON_DIRECTORY_ROOT
Lookup succeeds but the final target is not a directory
(for example a regular file or a symlink to a file),
or lookup reports
.B ENOTDIR
because a component prevents directory resolution.
Such an existing non\-directory PATH component cannot participate in normal
command lookup and is reported as a hazard (status
.BR 1 ),
not as a silent success and not as an operational error.
.B MISSING_ROOT
and
.B NON_DIRECTORY_ROOT
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.B GROUP_WRITABLE
The final directory target has
.B S_IWGRP
set.
Under
.B \-\-path
and
.BR \-\-command ,
the same code may also name a resolved executable
.B realpath
when that target has
.B S_IWGRP
set.
.TP
.B WORLD_WRITABLE
The final directory target has
.B S_IWOTH
set.
Under
.B \-\-path
and
.BR \-\-command ,
the same code may also name a resolved executable
.B realpath
when that target has
.B S_IWOTH
set.
.TP
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
Emitted under
.B \-\-path
and
.BR \-\-command
when a trusted\-owner check fails.
Trusted owners are UID 0 and the invoking real UID from
.BR getuid (2) .
For resolved executable targets the finding names the executable
.BR realpath
(symlink candidates use final\-target ownership).
For usable PATH directories under
.BR \-\-path ,
and for applicable components under
.BR \-\-command ,
the same trust rule also walks the directory
.B realpath
and each canonical ancestor through
.BR / ;
shared offending ancestors keep the lowest PATH index.
.B \-\-path
does not audit the process working directory for empty PATH fields;
under
.B \-\-command
an empty PATH field may audit the cwd chain
.RB ( "." )
when the applicability gate holds.
Explicit\-root mode never searches executables, never walks ancestors, and
never emits
.BR UNSAFE_OWNER .
.P
Lookup follows symbolic links like
.BR stat (2) ;
a symlink is not itself a hazard.
Directory permission findings use only the final directory target's
.B st_mode
bits.
Missing and non\-directory roots receive no permission finding.
A directory with both group\- and world\-write bits produces both findings.
Sticky, set\-ID, execute, read, owner, ACL, credential, and mount\-policy state
neither suppress nor create a finding.
Executable writability reuses
.B GROUP_WRITABLE
/
.B WORLD_WRITABLE
on the executable
.BR realpath ;
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
ranks after those permission codes for the same root.
Under
.BR \-\-path ,
shared\-taxonomy findings (including
.BR UNSAFE_OWNER )
precede all
.B SHADOWED
lines.
.B SHADOWED
names a later distinct executable
.B realpath
for a basename already won earlier in
.BR PATH .
Uniqueness is by
.RI ( command , winner realpath , shadow realpath ) :
exact duplicate tuples emit once, so repeated non\-winner components that
resolve to the same shadow
.B realpath
produce one row; genuinely distinct later realpaths still produce one row
each.
The first PATH\-order hit remains the winner.
A unique
.B SHADOWED
or
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
finding completes inspection with exit status
.B 1
and empty stderr on the successful hazard path.
To remediate, replace foreign\-owned PATH executables with root\-owned or
self\-owned trusted binaries, or remove the untrusted PATH entry;
.B pathaudit
does not
.B chown
files or edit
.BR PATH .
.P
In
.B \-\-command
mode, hazard applicability is narrowed to the query:
empty and relative PATH fields always remain cwd\-dependent hazards;
absolute
.B MISSING_ROOT
and
.B NON_DIRECTORY_ROOT
before a clean winner are omitted;
.B GROUP_WRITABLE
and
.B WORLD_WRITABLE
apply to directories that produced a
.B MATCH
and to writable directories that still precede the first
.B MATCH
(plant risk);
a writable absolute directory after the winner with no match for
.I NAME
is not reported.
The directory
.B realpath
and ancestor\-chain
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
walk uses that same match\-or\-plant\-risk gate.
An empty PATH field audits the process working directory chain
.RB ( "." )
under that gate
.RB ( \-\-path
does not).
Each
.B MATCH
target also receives executable writability codes and may receive
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
when the final\-target owner is neither UID 0 nor the invoking real UID.
.P
Each finding is one stdout line:
.B CODE
, a TAB, a quoted escaped root, and a newline.
Findings are ordered by raw root bytes, then original input position for
byte\-identical duplicates, then the fixed code rank above.
Every applicable code is emitted once per input.
.P
Exit status
.B 0
means inspection completed and no hazard was found
(in
.B \-\-command
mode,
.B MATCH
lines alone do not force status
.BR 1 ).
Status
.B 1
means inspection completed and at least one hazard was emitted
(including
.BR UNSAFE_OWNER ).
Status
.B 2
means usage failure, unset
.B PATH
in
.B \-\-path
or
.B \-\-command
mode, an invalid
.B \-\-command
.IR NAME ,
an input\-limit violation, a metadata error not classified as a hazard
(for example symlink loops or
.BR EACCES ),
allocation failure, or an output write/flush failure.
Limit and inspection failures are reject\-closed: buffered findings are
discarded and stdout remains empty.
.SH COMMAND QUERY
.B pathaudit \-\-command
.I NAME
is bounded command\-specific PATH risk inspection.
It walks PATH components left to right.
For each component it builds a candidate path
.RI ( NAME
alone when the component is empty;
otherwise
.IR dir/NAME )
and, when that path names a regular file with execute permission, appends one
.B MATCH
line whose value is the
.BR realpath (3)
of the candidate.
Non\-executable files and directories named like
.I NAME
are not matches.
Repeated PATH entries preserve separate match positions.
Shadowing is visible as multiple
.B MATCH
lines in PATH order.
.P
Stdout order is all
.B MATCH
lines first (PATH order), then applicable hazard lines (deterministic finding
order).
Other executables that share a PATH directory but have a different basename
are unrelated benign collisions and never appear.
.P
Diagnostics go only to stderr.
.B INVALID_COMMAND
includes the escaped
.IR NAME .
.B PATH_UNSET
does not append the usage synopsis.
.SH OUTPUT
Findings use the form
.BR CODE<TAB>"ESCAPED_ROOT" .
Command\-query matches use the form
.BR MATCH<TAB>"ESCAPED_PATH" .
Inside the quotes, printable ASCII bytes
.B 0x20
through
.B 0x7e
are emitted literally except
.B "
and
.B \e
which become
.B \e"
and
.BR \e\e ;
every other byte is uppercase
.BR \exHH .
An empty operand or empty PATH component is rendered as
.BR \&"" .
Diagnostics go only to stderr as
.B pathaudit: REASON
optionally followed by a quoted root or command name.
.B PATH_UNSET
does not append the usage synopsis.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Inspection completed; no hazard found.
Also used for successful
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version .
In
.B \-\-command
mode, successful matches with no applicable hazard still exit
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B 1
Inspection completed; at least one hazard emitted
(including empty
.B PATH
as one
.BR EMPTY_ROOT ,
an existing non\-directory component as
.BR NON_DIRECTORY_ROOT ,
.BR UNSAFE_OWNER ,
or
.BR SHADOWED ).
Suppressing a duplicate
.B SHADOWED
copy never turns a run that still has a unique shadow into status
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, unset
.B PATH
in
.B \-\-path
or
.B \-\-command
mode
.RB ( PATH_UNSET ),
invalid
.B \-\-command
.I NAME
.RB ( INVALID_COMMAND ),
limit violation, operational metadata error, allocation failure,
or stdout write/flush failure.
.SH RESOURCE LIMITS
.IP \(bu 3
Maximum roots or PATH components: 65\|536.
.IP \(bu 3
Maximum root or component length: 65\|536 bytes excluding the terminating NUL.
.IP \(bu 3
Maximum aggregate root/component bytes including terminating NULs: 1\|048\|576.
.P
In
.B \-\-path
and
.B \-\-command
modes those limits apply to components after colon splitting.
Overlong
.B \-\-command
candidate paths are treated as non\-matches rather than limit failures.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
pathaudit /tmp
pathaudit \-\- \-dash-root
pathaudit /safe /group-writable /missing
pathaudit \-\-path
pathaudit \-\-command ls
env \-u PATH pathaudit \-\-path
PATH=/safe:/tmp:/safe pathaudit \-\-path
PATH=/safe:/tmp pathaudit \-\-command tool
.fi
.RE
.P
The
.B env \-u PATH
example exits with status
.B 2
and prints
.B pathaudit: PATH_UNSET
on stderr with empty stdout.
A controlled
.B PATH
with a private directory and a world\-writable directory emits
.B WORLD_WRITABLE
for the writable component and exits with status
.BR 1 .
.B pathaudit \-\-command tool
emits
.B MATCH
lines only for the basename
.B tool
in PATH order, then applicable hazards for that query, without listing other
basenames in the same directories.
A foreign\-owned
.B MATCH
target also emits
.B UNSAFE_OWNER
naming the executable
.B realpath
and exits with status
.BR 1 .
